in this episode of cloud performance  atlas we help some global image  crunchers bring their costs down using  pre-emptive VMs will we get their  performance out of this world stay tuned  to find out sky eye is a research  organization whose focused on the health  of the planet by processing digital  imagery from satellites they came to me  with a massive problem as a seed funded  startup they were having trouble keeping  their core hours down since processing a  petabyte of image data on a regular  basis was a huge undertaking and was a  lot of costs their system grabs  satellite imagery and pushes the image  information to cloud storage while  pushing the metadata to datastore for  retrieval later they also have a  notification system set up when images  are uploaded to the GCS bucket which  creates a pub/sub message containing  details about where to find the image  this pub/sub message is then pulled by  an auto scaling instance group which  grabs the image data store information  and starts processing the image data all  the results are pushed to bigquery where  they can search sort and analyze the  results now at this point we ran down  the list of topics we checked how the  instance groups were performing what  their start-up time looked like and the  performance of their nodes individually  turns out one of their engineers was a  fan of our video series so all the stuff  that we would normally recommend was  already fixed thanks for watching which  meant that they weren't doing anything  wrong they just had a ton of work and  needed to keep the cost down which is  why I pointed them on the direction of  pre-emptive VMs pre-emptive VMs are the  same as regular instances except for one  key difference compute engine might  terminate these instances if it requires  access to those resources for other  tasks which can happen at any time now I  know what you're thinking that sounds  mega disruptive but they have one upside  by not guaranteeing indefinite uptime  Google is able to offer pre-emptive VMs  at a substantial discount to normal  instances which means they may not be a  fit for all of your architecture but  there may be parts of your cloud  application which they are ideal for for  sky-eye converting over to pre-emptive  VMs was pretty simple it started by  flipping the pre-emptive flag in their  instance template and then they made  modifications to handle the pre-emptive  events see both  or a PV M can be shut down it has to  signal your instance ahead of time  giving your app time to respond and  clear local data before things get lost  for the design that sky I was doing this  was really simple since they were doing  a pub/sub stream they just didn't  acknowledge the receipt and let another  vm in the group pick up that work and do  it on their behalf sky I shifted over  and recently completed a massive  experiment using almost 30,000 CPUs to  process 1 petabyte of imagery in just 16  hours all using pre-emptive VMS and  saving some serious coin  to learn more about the perf details of  these buckets check out the article  linked in the description below and  don't forget to check out the rest of  our cloud performance Atlas content  because when it comes to performance  every millisecond counts
 

